export default [
{slug:'school-timetable-weekly-printable',title:'Make a weekly school timetable that matches the real day',category:'learning',summary:'Build periods and breaks first, then place subjects and check the printable schedule against actual school hours.',tools:['school-timetable','study-plan','pomodoro'],sources:['printing','page'],sections:[
{title:'Start with time rather than subject colors',paragraphs:['A timetable becomes useful when it reflects the day people actually follow. Write down the opening time, period boundaries, lunch and any shorter breaks before arranging subjects. Include assembly or travel time when it affects the schedule. A beautiful grid with an impossible transition is still an unusable timetable.','The school timetable maker is a manual planning tool. It does not solve teacher availability, allocate rooms or guarantee that two classes do not need the same resource. Use it to express a schedule you have checked, rather than treating the generated page as an automated scheduling decision.']},
{title:'Build a small representative week',paragraphs:['Choose the school days and enter a row for each period or break. Use short subject labels that will fit in the printed cells. A label such as Science lab can be clearer than a long sentence, while a separate note can explain the room or equipment.','For example, four 45-minute lessons use 180 minutes. Add a 20-minute break and the block lasts 200 minutes. If it starts at 09:00, it ends at 12:20. Check these boundaries manually against the actual bell schedule; typing a time range into a label does not make the tool validate the day’s arithmetic.'],list:['Choose the days that belong in this timetable.','Enter periods and breaks in chronological order.','Fill subjects or activities for each day.','Use blank cells deliberately and label any special event.','Generate the landscape page and inspect all cell text.']},
{title:'Review the schedule from two perspectives',paragraphs:['Read each day vertically to check the learner’s experience. Then read each period across the week to spot repeated labels, missing breaks or an accidental copy into the wrong day. If rooms and teachers matter, compare this page with their separate schedules.','Long text can become difficult to read in a weekly grid. Shorten labels rather than reducing everything to tiny type. Avoid using color as the only sign that a period is a break or a special subject. A written label remains understandable in grayscale printing and for people who distinguish colors differently.']},
{title:'Keep an editable copy beside the printout',paragraphs:['Export the CSV as a simple record of the entries and the PDF for printing. A printed schedule is a snapshot: it does not update when you change a later version. Add a revision date or term label where appropriate and replace outdated copies in the places people consult them.','Select the correct paper and landscape orientation in print preview. Check a physical copy before making many copies. A personal study planner serves a different purpose: it can allocate independent practice around the fixed timetable. Keep the fixed school commitments visible before adding extra study sessions, and leave realistic time for meals, travel and rest.']}
]},
{slug:'print-kids-worksheets-purposeful-practice',title:'Choose a printable worksheet with one clear practice goal',category:'learning',summary:'Use alphabet, math, handwriting or coloring pages deliberately and check the exported PDF before printing.',tools:['kids-worksheets','word-search','school-timetable'],sources:['printing','page'],sections:[
{title:'Pick one activity for one purpose',paragraphs:['A worksheet is easier to use when it asks for one clear action. Alphabet recognition, arithmetic practice, handwriting and coloring are different activities. Choose the one that matches what the learner is practicing today, rather than printing every available page as a packet.','The worksheet maker provides four types. Alphabet pages combine letter practice and finding activities. Math pages generate arithmetic questions and a key. Handwriting pages use tracing text and writing guides. Coloring pages provide original outlined scenes. None of these pages assesses a child’s development or replaces individualized teaching.']},
{title:'Make the settings concrete',paragraphs:['For alphabet work, decide which letters to include and explain the task with one example. For handwriting, use short familiar words and make sure the chosen type style is appropriate for the learner’s school. A faint tracing line can guide a pencil, but it is not evidence that a particular stroke sequence is being taught.','For math, begin with a small, appropriate number range and the intended operation. Solve a few generated examples yourself and check the key. If subtraction permits negative answers, decide whether that belongs in the lesson. Difficulty should come from the learning goal, rather than cramped spacing or unclear instructions.'],list:['Choose one worksheet type.','Set the letter, word list or arithmetic options.','Generate and inspect the full page.','Read the instructions aloud once and remove ambiguity.','Print the learner page and keep any answer key separate.']},
{title:'Use the answer key as a review tool',paragraphs:['An answer key helps an adult verify the activity and helps a learner check completed work. It should not be mixed into the learner’s first page by accident. The combined PDF can include more than one page, so inspect the page count and print selection.','Check how many questions fit comfortably. Large pencil movements, generous margins and clear line spacing may matter more than maximizing the number of exercises. For coloring, choose a scene with shapes the learner can see comfortably; a detailed page is not automatically more educational.']},
{title:'Print without introducing new obstacles',paragraphs:['A4 is 210 × 297 mm, while US Letter has different proportions. Match the selected page size and printer paper. The direct PDF download avoids the need to capture a screenshot of the browser. PNG and SVG are useful when assembling another document, but still need an appropriate layout and print scale.','Print one sample and check line visibility, margins and writing space. Keep the editable word list or settings if you expect to repeat the activity. Offer a simpler page, larger print or a non-writing alternative when needed. Use the finished sheet as a starting point for conversation and feedback; the generator cannot know whether the activity is suitable for an individual learner.']}
]},
{slug:'habit-calendar-streaks-local-records',title:'Use a habit calendar without letting a streak become the whole goal',category:'planning',summary:'Record a few clear actions, interpret streaks accurately and keep a backup of optional local records.',tools:['habit-tracker','pomodoro','date-difference'],sources:['storage'],sections:[
{title:'Define what a completed day means',paragraphs:['A useful habit record starts with an action you can recognize. “Read for ten minutes” is easier to mark consistently than “be productive.” Choose a small number of habits so entering the record remains quick. The calendar accepts up to ten names, but using fewer can make the page easier to review.','The marks are self-reported. They record what you choose to enter and do not verify behavior, measure health or diagnose motivation. Decide whether a day counts before looking at the streak number. If the definition changes during the month, note the change outside the calendar rather than pretending the records are directly comparable.']},
{title:'Understand the three numbers',paragraphs:['Marked days count all checked dates for the selected habit and month. The longest run counts the largest uninterrupted sequence of marked days in that month. The ending run looks backward from today, or yesterday when today is unmarked, for the current month; for another month it uses the end of that month.','For example, marks on days 1, 2, 4, 5 and 6 give five completed days and a longest run of three. They do not mean the habit happened for six days in a row. A blank date means no mark is recorded, rather than proof of failure or a judgment about the person.']},
{title:'Keep recording simple',paragraphs:['Choose the month, enter distinct habit names and update the calendar. Select one habit and click the dates that belong in the record. Review the displayed label before marking several days, especially when switching between habits.','Reset this month clears marks for the selected month. The interface offers an immediate Undo reset so an accidental reset can be reversed during that session. Export records before major changes if the history matters. A printed page is a snapshot rather than a synchronized record.'],list:['Start with one to three specific habit names.','Mark dates near the time you complete the action.','Review weekly totals and patterns, not just the longest streak.','Export JSON when you want an independent record.','Print a monthly grid if a paper reminder is useful.']},
{title:'Make an explicit storage choice',paragraphs:['Without optional saving, records live in the current page session. Save on this device uses browser local storage. That storage belongs to the site origin and browser profile; it is not an account, cloud sync or permanent backup. Clearing site data or changing browsers can remove access to the entries.','Use a device you control for personal records and keep an exported copy when appropriate. A streak can be a convenient visual cue, but it is not the only sign of progress. If the record becomes discouraging, review the action’s size, frequency and purpose instead of treating a broken sequence as a reason to abandon the activity.']}
]},
{slug:'room-floor-plan-furniture-clearance',title:'Make a simple room plan before moving furniture',category:'planning',summary:'Measure rectangular footprints, compare positions and understand the difference between no overlap and usable clearance.',tools:['room-planner','unit-converter','unit-converter'],sources:['printing'],sections:[
{title:'Measure the room and the furniture',paragraphs:['A room plan is useful when all dimensions refer to the same thing. Measure usable interior width and depth, then measure each furniture footprint. Include handles or protruding parts when they affect placement. Use metres consistently; 140 cm becomes 1.4 m, not 140 in a field labeled metres.','The planner models a rectangular room and rectangular furniture. It does not model structural work, irregular walls, fire access, electrical services or building requirements. Use it for a first arrangement and verify the real space before buying furniture or moving anything heavy.']},
{title:'Read positions from the top-left corner',paragraphs:['Each item has a left coordinate, a top coordinate, width and depth. An item at x = 0.2 m and y = 0.3 m begins 20 cm from the left wall and 30 cm from the top wall in the drawing. Rotation by 90 degrees exchanges its displayed width and depth.','For a 4 × 3 m room, a 1.4 × 2 m bed at x = 0.2 and y = 0.2 occupies x = 0.2–1.6 and y = 0.2–2.2. That fits inside the room. A desk beginning at x = 1.5 can overlap the bed even if both items individually fit within the walls.']},
{title:'Use overlap warnings as a first check',paragraphs:['The tool checks whether rectangular footprints overlap. A clean result means those rectangles do not intersect under the entered measurements. It does not mean there is enough room to open a wardrobe door, pull out a chair or move safely around the room.','Enter furniture through the table or drag a rectangle in the result. Dragging updates the stored coordinates and regenerates the plan so later exports reflect the move. The background grid is spaced at half a metre; small adjustments should still be checked against the numeric fields.'],list:['Enter the room’s measured width and depth.','Add furniture names and measured dimensions.','Set positions or drag items inside the room.','Review overlap warnings and revise the layout.','Check door swings, chair movement and walkways separately.']},
{title:'Take the plan back to the room',paragraphs:['Download the printable plan and compare it with the actual space. A rectangular room’s area is width multiplied by depth: 4 × 3 m gives 12 m². That area does not tell you whether a particular arrangement is comfortable. The shape of the remaining paths matters as well.','Optional local saving keeps entries in this browser, and a JSON backup keeps a separate copy. Neither is a professional measured survey. Re-measure tight spaces and check product dimensions before purchasing. If an arrangement affects structural elements, access or regulated work, obtain appropriate professional input rather than relying on a simplified diagram.']}
]},
{slug:'seating-chart-capacity-manual-shuffle',title:'Build a seating chart with capacities you can verify',category:'planning',summary:'Keep guest labels unique, reserve explicit assignments and use fill or shuffle only for the decisions you intend.',tools:['seating-chart','random-picker','packing-checklist'],sources:['storage','printing'],sections:[
{title:'Make the guest list unambiguous',paragraphs:['Two people named Sam need distinct labels in a seating list. Use a surname, initial or another identifier that the event organizer can understand without exposing unnecessary private information. The seating tool requires unique labels so a repeated name cannot silently become two uncertain assignments.','Enter each table with its capacity. A label such as Table 1 | 4 means four seats, not four additional seats after manual assignments. Check the venue’s actual layout and any accessibility or service requirements separately. The tool does not contact guests, send invitations or know relationship preferences.']},
{title:'Choose the assignment mode deliberately',paragraphs:['Manual mode uses the table entered beside each guest. Fill blank assignments preserves explicit table entries and then allocates the unassigned rows to available seats. Shuffle all assignments redistributes everyone within capacities. These modes answer different questions, so review the chosen one before generating.','For example, three tables with capacities 4, 4 and 6 provide 14 seats. Twelve guests leave two unused places. If five guests are manually assigned to the first four-seat table, the plan still fails despite spare capacity elsewhere. The specific table capacity matters, not just the grand total.'],list:['Enter the table names and real capacities.','Use unique, concise guest labels.','Add any required manual table assignments.','Select manual, fill blanks or shuffle all.','Generate and check every table’s list before exporting.']},
{title:'Use reproducible shuffling for a draft',paragraphs:['The shuffle set acts as a seed, so the same inputs and set can reproduce a draft. Change it to try another arrangement. This is useful for classroom grouping or a low-stakes event draft, but it is not an audited lottery and does not encode fairness or social preferences.','Review the result with a human organizer. Keep families, interpreters, mobility needs and other relevant constraints in mind without putting sensitive explanations into a public chart. If a particular guest must stay at a certain table, use manual assignments or fill mode instead of expecting the shuffle to infer that rule.']},
{title:'Export the right amount of information',paragraphs:['The printable cards paginate long table lists, and the CSV provides a straightforward guest-to-table mapping. Guest and table labels are bounded so they remain visible on the cards. Read the final printout for spelling and compare the guest count with the source list.','Save on this device is optional and uses browser storage. Export a backup if the plan matters, and remove local records on a shared computer when the event is over. Keep organizer notes in a separate private document and print only the information guests need. A seating chart is a snapshot, so label and distribute revisions carefully when assignments change.']}
]},
{slug:'pantry-list-package-dates-inventory',title:'Keep a pantry list without treating dates as a food-safety verdict',category:'planning',summary:'Record quantities, storage locations and package-label dates while keeping calendar comparisons separate from safety decisions.',tools:['pantry-tracker','recipe-scaler','unit-price'],sources:['storage'],sections:[
{title:'Record what you can actually verify',paragraphs:['A pantry list works best as a simple inventory: what the item is, how much remains and where it is stored. Use consistent units such as kilograms, cans or packets. “Rice, 2 kg, cupboard” is more useful than a vague entry called food. Update the quantity after using or restocking an item.','The date field copies a date from the package. It does not interpret the manufacturer’s wording or assess storage history. Different labels can describe different things, and a date by itself cannot establish whether a particular food is safe to eat. Keep the label and follow the package’s instructions and appropriate guidance.']},
{title:'Use calendar comparisons only for organization',paragraphs:['If an entered date is five days away, the tool reports five days before that date. If it passed three days ago, it reports three days after the entered date. Those are arithmetic comparisons, rather than fresh, safe or unsafe judgments. A missing date remains visibly unentered.','Do not invent a date for an unlabeled item just to make the table complete. Add a descriptive note to the item name if necessary and consult the original packaging. The tool cannot account for temperature, contamination, damaged packaging, allergies or a power interruption.']},
{title:'Build a short inventory you can maintain',paragraphs:['Start with one shelf or storage location. Enter each item and its remaining quantity, then compare the result with the actual shelf. Separating opened and unopened packages may be useful if they need different handling, but avoid creating so many rows that you stop updating the list.'],list:['Name items clearly and choose a consistent unit.','Record the current quantity and physical location.','Copy a package-label date only when one is available.','Review the sorted list for planning and shopping.','Export a CSV or JSON backup when needed.'],paragraphsAfter:['For example, if a recipe needs 500 g of rice and the list says 2 kg, convert both to the same unit before planning: 2 kg is 2000 g, leaving 1500 g after that use. The list still needs a real-world check before cooking, because someone else may have used the rice since the last update.']},
{title:'Keep planning and safety decisions separate',paragraphs:['The list can reduce duplicate shopping and help you see which ingredients to consider using. It cannot decide what anyone should eat. Do not rely on color, sorting or a calculated day count as a substitute for the actual label and storage guidance.','Optional local saving stays in this browser and can be cleared by browser settings. It is not a household sharing service or a permanent record. Export a backup if the inventory is valuable, and use a separate shared process if several people need to update it. When scaling a recipe, check the ingredient unit and the recipe’s instructions rather than simply assuming every item is interchangeable by weight.']}
]},
{slug:'monthly-budget-planned-actual',title:'Build a monthly budget with planned and actual amounts',category:'money',summary:'Separate the plan from the record, check the leftover arithmetic and keep all rows in one currency.',tools:['budget-planner','subscription-tracker','cash-flow'],sources:['budget','cashTiming'],sections:[
{title:'Choose one period and one currency',paragraphs:['A budget comparison becomes confusing when a monthly income is mixed with weekly expenses or amounts in several currencies. Choose a period, use one currency and translate recurring costs into that period consistently. The currency label describes the values; it does not perform an exchange-rate conversion.','Use income available for that period rather than an uncertain future receipt. If income varies, make the assumption explicit and revise it when the actual amount is known. This planner performs arithmetic and displays the difference. It does not decide an appropriate spending level or provide individualized financial advice.']},
{title:'Keep planned and actual values distinct',paragraphs:['Planned amounts describe what you expect to allocate. Actual amounts record what happened. Enter both for each category rather than replacing the plan whenever a receipt arrives. The difference helps you find where your assumptions need revision.','For an example income of NPR 50,000, suppose planned rent is 15,000, food 12,000, travel 4,000 and other costs 9,000. Planned spending totals 40,000, leaving 10,000. If actual amounts are 15,000, 13,500, 4,500 and 8,000, actual spending is 41,000 and actual leftover is 9,000. The 1000 difference is a comparison, not an explanation of why it happened.']},
{title:'Use categories you can update',paragraphs:['Start with a manageable set of categories and combine very small expenses when that helps consistent recording. Include periodic bills in a deliberate way; a yearly renewal can be tracked separately in the subscription tool and translated into an average monthly planning amount.'],list:['Enter the period’s income and currency.','Add category names with planned and actual amounts.','Update the results and check the totals.','Investigate differences using your own records.','Export the table before beginning the next period.'],paragraphsAfter:['A positive leftover is the difference under the entered rows. It is not necessarily free-to-spend cash if a bill, debt payment, transfer or expense is missing. A negative result identifies an arithmetic shortfall in the entered plan; it does not prescribe which category should change.']},
{title:'Distinguish a budget from cash timing',paragraphs:['A monthly budget can balance while a bank balance goes short before payday. If an important payment leaves before income arrives, use the cash-flow tool to model the order of receipts and payments. Profit, budget surplus and available cash answer different questions.','Save on this device is optional. Browser storage can be cleared and is not a secure financial vault or a synchronized account. Export a backup if the entries matter, avoid sensitive account numbers in category labels and verify important amounts against statements or receipts. Revisit the model as your circumstances change rather than treating one month’s output as a permanent plan.']}
]}
];
